+2.1 The maximal bosonic direct-sum maps are closed. For example, if $\Phi^{(j)}\to\Phi$ and $a(h)\Phi^{(j)}\to\Theta$ in Fock norm, boundedness of each sector map gives coordinatewise $\Theta_{n-1}=a_n(h)\Phi_n$; since $\Theta$ is square-summable, the defining sum for $D(a(h))$ is finite and $a(h)\Phi=\Theta$. The same coordinate argument applies to $a^\dagger(h)$. Truncating a vector and its image after sector $N$ shows $\mathcal D_{\rm fin}$ is a graph core for each. For the adjoint, testing against vectors supported in input sector $n$ forces the output coordinate in sector $n+1$ to be $a_{n+1}^\dagger(h)\Phi_n$; square-summability of these coordinates is exactly the displayed domain of $a^\dagger(h)$. Conversely, if that sum is finite, the sector pairing of step 1.2 and Cauchy–Schwarz in the direct sum give the adjoint identity for every vector in $D(a(h))$. Thus $a(h)^*=a^\dagger(h)$ with the displayed maximal domains. Testing against single output sectors in the reverse direction gives $(a^\dagger(h))^*=a(h)$ by the identical coordinate and square-summability argument. [step 1.1, step 1.2, F1, F3, F5, algebra]
 
 2.2 For a symmetric elementary tensor $v_1\vee\cdots\vee v_n:=P_{s,n}(v_1\otimes\cdots\otimes v_n)$, contraction and creation give $a(h)(v_1\vee\cdots\vee v_n)=n^{-1/2}\sum_{j=1}^n\langle h,v_j\rangle_2 \,v_1\vee\cdots\widehat{v_j}\cdots\vee v_n$ and $a^\dagger(k)(v_1\vee\cdots\vee v_n)=\sqrt{n+1}\, P_{s,n+1}(k\otimes v_1\otimes\cdots\otimes v_n)$, by expanding the permutation averages [F2]. In the composition $a(h)a^\dagger(k)$, the contraction term for the newly inserted $k$ is $\langle h,k\rangle_2(v_1\vee\cdots\vee v_n)$; each of the other $n$ contraction terms is exactly the corresponding term of $a^\dagger(k)a(h)$. Hence their difference is $\langle h,k\rangle_2I$. Symmetry of $P_{s,n}$ makes two creations commute; two contractions commute because contracting distinct tensor slots in either order gives the same sum. Finite elementary symmetric tensors are dense in each sector and the sector maps are bounded there, so the identities hold on all of $\mathcal D_{\rm fin}$. [F1, F2, step 1.2, algebra]
 
